WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.780
Flying Dragon, The Secret Scroll, Kung Fu has never been this real.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.980
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.780
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.260
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.340
I don't really have...

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.480
Wait, what? Sorry, I was just going to say, what about real Kung Fu?

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.320
Isn't that as real, at least, as this?

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:48.080
Yeah, I guess, like, Kung Fu is already real, so it doesn't have to, like, get more real, right?

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.980
Right. Sorry, go on.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.920
No, no, no. I guess that's a good...

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.000
I didn't have any topic prepared, so that's a good starting point for us here.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.020
We're going to slowly develop the episode from here.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:04.520
And I think that that is the best note to start on, is that Kung Fu is real, and people should know about it.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.280
Dragons, not real, and have arms and legs.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.160
They don't always have arms, but they usually have legs.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.740
God, you do this on purpose.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.880
I don't know what you mean, Mike.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.560
I have no idea either.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.780
I think it's time to start talking about video games.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.320
I'm somewhat familiar with the idea of video games, more than I am familiar with Kung Fu.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.520
But we've played our fair share of Kung Fu video games.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:38.360
And, you know, Flying Dragon, The Secret Scroll is, like, a mixture of two different kinds of games.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.420
It's half-side-scrolling action games, similar to, like, Final Fight, or...

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.720
I was thinking, for NES-specific, Kid Nicky Radical Ninja.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.780
If anybody wants to do, like, a deep pullback.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.820
Like, that game was very similar to this game.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:58.660
But then there's also other stages, where it's just basically a fighting game, similar to Street Fighter.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.700
Now, granted, it's not exactly like Street Fighter, because Street Fighter 2 hasn't perfected the fighting game system.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.100
So they still have to figure out a way to handle that, and we'll get into that.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.780
But I think it's interesting that we continue to see this trend of, like...

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.700
Specifically in 1989, of games being, like, no, you can't just be, like, one thing anymore.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:22.460
You're either a bunch of minigames, or you're, like, mixing or fusing genres together.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:31.460
Nothing really to add to that, but I was just wondering if you guys were expecting this to be, you know, more of a traditional fighter game...

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:37.340
Or, you know, an action-adventure game similar to, you know, the ones I referenced.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.820
I was more expecting the side-scrolling element.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:51.400
Just because, you know, I'd never heard of this game, and it just sort of seemed like, oh, here's another side-scroller, but here's another kung fu-flavored one.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:03:01.580
And in the beginning, it sort of fell into that expectation, and I guess it would have more so if I didn't read the manual in here...

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.700
...and, like, read about what I was going to get into next.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:10.460
But, yeah, I was originally thinking this would be just that one thing.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.340
Yeah, I was thinking the same thing.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.000
Even though, you know, at the beginning they do allude to the fact that you're, like, headed to a tournament.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.840
So I sort of, at that point, was wondering what this tournament was going to be.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:26.380
But I'll say I was expecting it to be a tournament where you take everything you've done in the side-scrolling levels...

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.780
...and now you're...it's like an evolution of it or another version of that.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.880
But really, I was surprised that there's just two kind of gameplay styles that aren't really that related.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.260
Yeah, they're actually so far apart in terms of, like, play style and just, you know, the flow of the game...

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:49.180
...that they actually make it a point to, when you get to the fighting part of it, to be like, actually, you have no idea what to do.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.300
We need to give you a tutorial to explain what to do.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.340
And they didn't do that at all for the side-scrolling action part, right?

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.400
They weren't like, hey, we should teach people about, like...

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.380
Punching and kicking.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.860
Right, right. Or just, like, you know, what you need to do, right?

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.920
Of, like, you need to hit these tiki things or...

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.340
I can't remember what they are, but, like, these boxes and then they'll...

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.440
...things will appear from them.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.840
Or you need to defeat, like, all five bosses.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.720
Now, granted, that stuff's probably covered in the manual.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.540
I didn't look at it.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:23.000
But it's just a weird flow of something to be like, yeah, we understand that everybody knows how to do this part of the game.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.180
And we're going to give you that first.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:33.700
But then when we get to the other element of our game, we actually have to, like, stop and teach you, with an actual controller on screen, just the idea of doing this.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:34.500
That was crazy.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.940
Like, this is the first, like, on-screen tutorial I've ever seen, I think.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.400
And it's done pretty fluidly.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.360
I know that it stops the action in its tracks, but, I mean, like, it's intuitive.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:58.920
It definitely is more useful than a poorly worded manual entry, and you just sort of have to parse the rest yourself.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.940
Like, I thought that that was done relatively well.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:18.880
Yeah, I really wanted to kind of praise that element of this game as well, because it does feel like, for the most part, if not always, we've so far had to go back to the manual anytime we were, like, confused about the controls or there was some new gameplay element introduced.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:24.780
And like you said, Sean, sometimes that didn't translate perfectly to exactly, you know, you had to sort of figure it out.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:33.820
And this did a really good job of teaching you, not only teaching you exactly how to do it, but, like, you know, there's a little bit of a, I don't want to say a learning curve.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.700
It's a pretty simple concept, but, like, at least a learning curve is in terms of your muscle memory.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.800
And it gives you a good, like, the tutorial doesn't just teach you exactly how to do it.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:53.280
It gives you some time to learn it, so you feel like when you're done with that tutorial, you've kind of upped your skill a little bit to where you feel like you're equipped to go on and actually fight the tournaments.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.940
So I thought they did a good job with that.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:06:09.740
Yeah, and I think it also just shows how new of an idea this is to, like, video games that they felt the need to, you know, stop all the gameplay and action and progress and be like, here's how you're going to do these things.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:18.800
And, you know, that's really the biggest takeaway for me was just that, you know, it hasn't been perfected yet how we do, like, one-on-one combat, right?

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:24.280
Like, we've seen wrestling games handle it one way, other games handle it via, like, menu systems.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:34.020
Nobody's quite figured out yet, like, the idea of you and your opponent having different sets of moves and each having, like, a health bar that corresponds.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.660
But I think this comes really close.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:43.600
I actually question, you know, like, they came so close, but they didn't because they didn't figure it out, right?

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.320
It's like some people will be disappointed.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.080
But I don't think this is, like, the wrong way to go either.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:56.060
So if you don't mind, I'm going to actually, like, push aside the side-scrolling levels for right now, even though the game starts with them, because I feel like that's just something easier to get into.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:06.420
I want to talk about the fighting game levels, because since this isn't your traditional fighting game, you have these targets that indicate what kind of button input you need to do.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:21.020
What did you guys think of that idea versus, you know, traditional Street Fighter, Tekken, Mortal Kombat-style games where, you know, you're in complete control of the inputs that you do, but you kind of need to, like, memorize these awesome inputs.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:28.780
Whereas this game says, like, you don't really have control over what input you're going to do next, but you have to remember these patterns.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:41.860
And then by doing these things with the limited controller that you have, right, you only have the D-pad and the two buttons, you'll be able to do, you know, more awesome things than you'd be able to if we just gave you one-button punches, one-button kicks.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:51.480
Yeah, I mean, it's definitely approaching, like, the quote-unquote fighting game from a different angle than we're used to.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:08:11.660
It's almost more like a Simon Says slash rhythm game sort of thing where, yeah, these targets appear on your body and your opponent's body, and they sort of telegraph where you should hit as, like, a weak spot or where your opponent is going to hit that you need to defend.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.360
And I think it still works.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.680
It works pretty well as an idea.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:31.880
And then when you're actually fighting and you sort of get into that rhythm, like, even the idea of, like, a combo sort of shows itself.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:41.820
It's a very simple one, but, like, even your standard inputs are kind of a combo because you have to target, like, these different parts of your opponent's body.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:52.000
But then you get slightly more complex ones where, like, with your sort of grab them and throw them over your head sort of thing.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:09:09.620
So there's a lot of elements that sort of will evolve as the fighting game genre evolves, but also stuff that'll just completely get thrown away, like this whole, like, they show you a target and you have to hit it sort of thing.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.700
But it's still very unique.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:20.320
Yeah, I thought the target thing, it helped to bring a lot to the table, but there was also, I think it compromised a lot of other things, too.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:38.980
So for one, because they're trying to kind of, I feel like, capture the spirit of, you know, of a martial arts, you know, sparring one-on-one match, this target system kind of gives you control over things like, okay, where do I want my guard to be?

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.820
Do I want to be blocking high, middle, low?

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:41.900
Where do I want to kick?

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:42.900
Like, where is he blocking?

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:53.580
Where it's like, I guess that translates decently to what it would be like to be in an actual, like, martial arts fight where you're thinking of, like, okay, he's dropped his guard, I go for the head.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.820
Okay, his guard's up, sweep the legs, whatever.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.340
So that's nice.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:19.060
And as, you know, just the way that it presents itself where these targets appear and you have to hit the target as it appears or you see where they're aiming and you have to block before they hit you, it lends itself to having a nice, fluid, what feels like a martial arts style fight as opposed to just, like, punch, punch, punch, punch.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.780
It's like a little more of, like, a dance, you know?

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.360
And that's nice. I think that that works and you really get into a rhythm as you're playing.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:34.400
What I think it loses is that even though you get more control over exactly where you're hitting and stuff, you lose pretty much all agency in the battle.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:38.520
You know, it's all about doing what it says is the best move at the right time.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:39.380
It's all about timing.

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:46.840
It is almost more of a rhythm game in the sense that it's just kind of a series of almost quick time events.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:53.100
Yeah. And when you describe it as a dance, like, those targets really just are your choreography.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:59.480
Like, yeah, you're not making any strategic decision about I should bait and then hit.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.240
You're just sort of doing what the game tells you to do.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:13.380
Exactly. And I think that that, for me, over time, started to slowly outweigh the appreciation I had for how cool it felt to fight this way.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.260
After a while, I was like, okay, I'm kind of bored now.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:16.720
I'm just doing what the game tells me.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:17.920
The game is playing me.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:31.740
Whoa. The fighting game that we've also compared to a rhythm game, Mike Tyson's Punch-Out, you know, that game, completely different graphical style that they were able to pull it off.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:42.520
But, like, imagine if instead of these, like, great animation tells that you have to look out for and, you know, subtle tricks in the audience or flashes or stuff like that.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:50.360
Imagine if instead they just, like, had a big dot over the fist when it was about to, like, punch this way you knew to dodge, right?

00:11:50.360 --> 00:12:01.380
It's, like, it's kind of like that in a way, but I don't know how, because this is, you know, a 2D from the side action fighting game.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:21.660
I don't know how they would be able to communicate, like, such great tells in the combat that it wouldn't just be reduced to the kind of combat that you get during the side-scrolling action stages, you know, where you're just, like, constantly just hitting punch and kick and hoping your punch and kicks outweigh the opponents, you know, and it just becomes like a spamming battle.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:39.680
So, because they had to come up with some kind of system to, like, create a more nuanced fighting system where it's actually like, you know, hey, you're responsible for both blocking and attacking, dependent on what, you know, what we show you on screen.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:50.440
I'm not sure how else they could have pulled that off, but at the same time, you know, I'm not ready to applaud that either because I do agree that, you know, you are just answering their commands.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:58.080
But also, like, you're less inclined to just, like, experiment in general, not even because you can't, but, like, I don't know about you guys.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.680
I didn't even think I could move.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:04.620
Like, I found out later on you can move, but, like, I couldn't think of a reason for why I'd really want to move.

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:10.540
There's no, like, grand reason for, you know, for space control or anything like that.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:17.020
Like, you still have to, when the dots finally do show up in the locations they do, you still have to answer the same call.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:27.820
Yeah. So, I mean, in terms of movement, like, yeah, it's not really well utilized because there's, like, you can dodge out of the way of certain attacks that have enough, like, lead time.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:33.380
But the thing about this game is I agree with you.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:45.100
Like, this was put into place because having it be more of, like, a standard fighter in, like, the standard word that we're thinking of.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.920
Like, the controls just aren't there.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.980
Like, even though you're being told what to do, they still kind of feel sticky.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.500
And I don't know if you, I guess it's kind of subjective.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:13:59.940
Maybe you guys didn't feel that way.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:14.840
But to me, they felt kind of sticky because the other solution, like, just playing armchair designer that I can think of to allow for more information to be given to you that isn't just an explicit target.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.160
Um, could be solved by just zooming in more.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:21.620
And that would make movement even less important.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.700
Um, jumping and stuff less important.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:36.580
And so that, like, these larger sprites that you're seeing for, uh, yourself and your opponent could have more telegraphing information or, uh, just as a tell.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:40.280
Work as a tell or, uh, something along those lines.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:50.620
But I understand that, like, if this is how the game feels, um, in its current state, then I don't think that just blowing it up would make it feel any better.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.500
Um, so I don't know.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:15:00.520
I guess I'm just sort of rambling about, like, the wider design of the game, uh, in terms of, like, why they went that way.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:10.680
Yeah, it's an interesting idea, though, the idea of, of blowing it up and then taking away the actual, like, icons on the screen and making the tells actual tells.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:18.520
Right? Because, you know, ever since, ever since Mike brought up Punch-Out!, I've been thinking about that where I'm like, why do I like Punch-Out!

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:19.820
so much better than I like this?

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:26.840
Because it is technically, if you really want to read into it, it is an indicator on the screen that tells you what to do.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:33.660
But there's something, one, about the immersion of something like Punch-Out!, where it's like you feel like you're actually fighting that person.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:39.000
And two, it's a little more complex than just, than just the dot telling you where to hit.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:43.180
So it's like, you know, it's a little more, like, real-life decision-making based on what you're seeing.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.540
Well, obviously, it's still a pattern.

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:51.420
But, like, so are almost every boss battle in any modern video game you play is all pattern recognition.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:53.660
And I think that, you know, it's like Punch-Out!

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.200
or something like what you're describing, Sean, about a possible way to do this,

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:02.700
is getting closer to just complex patterns instead of a pattern of blue dot, red dot.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:07.400
You know, where it's just, like, literally, press this button now, press this button now.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.500
That's what makes this feel, to me, like I'm not thinking.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.560
You know, the game is thinking for me.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:21.580
Yeah, it's important to remember that the dot here isn't just telling you, like, oh, hit their stomach or hit them in the head.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:26.240
It's telling you, just based on the way that the game, you know, sets up the tutorial for you, too,

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.620
it's telling you which buttons to press on your controller.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:34.860
So you don't even get the choice now of, like, they're saying go for the head and I want to do a strong kick.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:41.600
Like, no, you have to do the same exact inputs that a dot on that person's head means versus a dot on your head.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:46.980
Like, they're deciding what controller input you do, so you're just reacting.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:55.660
And even in the, you know, as the game continues, any additional things they add to the fighting,

00:16:55.880 --> 00:17:01.000
like they add some additional special moves and eventually there's an elimination round and stuff like that,

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.760
it's actually all just story progress.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:11.160
It doesn't actually enhance the actual fights any further because, specifically for the elimination round, right,

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:15.440
they show you this great, like, bracket of best of eight or whatever, right,

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.420
and, like, you move up in the tournament.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:21.980
And, you know, that's great and compelling as, like, wow, I got to make my way through this.

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:29.180
But every match in this game is an elimination round thing where, like, if you lose, you lose, and that's game over.

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:30.920
It's just window dressing the whole tree.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.240
Right, exactly. It's just dressing it up.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.340
But you could argue that the special moves are just doing that too, really.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:42.180
They're not, like, you know, there's that one where you do, like, a flying kick in the air where you're spinning around

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:43.480
and then you come down and hit them.

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:50.080
That doesn't actually change anything in the game other than now you know this technique,

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.480
but you can still only use it when we call for it, you know?

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:59.900
Yeah. And I think another important thing to mention with these dots is since it's reactionary,

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:03.680
it's kind of demanding on the reactionary side.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:07.300
I don't think it's, like, so fast that it's impossible by any means.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:09.200
And you don't want to be perfect, right?

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:10.560
That'd be kind of a boring game too.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:13.300
You want to make a mistake every now and again, so you don't want it to be, like,

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:14.740
you have all the time in the world.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:23.040
However, the amount of times that you have to, you know, react to these dots on an individual fight level

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.300
is just, like, that's a lot in itself.

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:30.960
But then to just continue to have to be on edge for this kind of thing,

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:36.560
like, I don't know if this dot system is really pulling off the longer you play it.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.600
I don't know if you guys feel that way about the reaction times,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:44.960
but it's just, like, as soon as you strike or block what they want you to do,

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.320
there's immediately a new dot on screen requiring the next set of inputs.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:55.620
Yeah. And, like, again, like, I think we've been, the way we've been pitching this game,

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:58.500
it sort of sounds like it plays itself and it's super easy.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:00.760
And what you just said is why it's not,

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:08.640
because while it is more just a test of reaction time and not so much fighting strategy,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.900
that is still very challenging.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:15.900
One could argue that it's not a very engaging way to be challenging, but you're right.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:22.160
Like, as the fights progress, it becomes a lot harder to sort of keep up

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.480
and to dodge as well as you should.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.080
So, yeah, I agree.

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:28.640
It's demanding.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:34.040
Yeah, I think the other thing that makes it, that still makes it challenging is that,

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:38.960
so I know I'm going to get this backwards or somehow messed up,

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.860
but something to the effect of when you're defending,

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:48.000
you have to just press the D-pad button in the direction you're defending,

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.900
but when you're attacking, you have to press the D-pad plus A

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:52.740
in the direction you want to attack on them,

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:54.180
which sounds pretty simple,

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:58.940
but it does become a little bit of, like, a pat your head, rub your stomach situation sometimes,

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:00.140
where you're like, wait, what am I doing?

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.900
So, like, you get into this rhythm and it feels really good,

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:04.980
and then when you slip out, like, once, suddenly you're like,

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.120
ah, like, you're kind of, like, caught on your back foot for a second.

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:13.080
And, you know, that part, you know, that element of it is cool at first, you know,

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:18.240
but it's just, I don't think it's interesting enough to hold my attention

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:19.960
for the amount of time it wants me to do it.

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:24.700
Yeah, it's kind of necessary for them to break up these fights

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.060
with these side-scrolling levels,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:30.060
because otherwise that would be the whole game, right?

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:34.180
It's just constantly, like, how much better, you know,

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:36.760
I think Sean put it perfectly when he said it's like Simon says,

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:40.920
because, you know, how long can you keep up with that?

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:42.900
Like, how long is that interesting?

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.480
Even if the animations change,

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:47.000
if the core concept remains the same forever,

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.020
it just becomes harder to go on.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:21:04.940
Right, right. Did you guys try the pause strategy at all?

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:06.880
The pause strategy?

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.360
Sounds like a strategy I would like.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.860
Yeah, I'm surprised, Joe, that you didn't know about it,

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.160
but in order to really make my way through this game

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.640
and, you know, just keep my interest levels up,

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:24.060
I found on GameFAQs that there is a whole strategy to the fighting

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:25.240
based around pausing,

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:29.840
and that is that as soon as you defend or attack, you know,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:30.940
the dot on screen,

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.260
because the next one pops up,

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.520
you can actually pause the screen,

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:37.000
the dot remains,

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:40.080
and now you know the next queue up attack.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:41.680
Oh, that sounds so fun.

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:43.300
No, it's not fun.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.760
It's not fun. I'm saying in order for me to continue on with the game,

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.160
you know, in the later levels and everything,

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:53.280
it's a strategy that is not fun at all,

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.040
but I also just can't believe that

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.360
they would allow the dot to remain on the pause.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:00.200
Like, it seems like such an easy thing to just, like,

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:03.700
if you pause, just, like, remove even just that one sprite

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:04.900
or just cover it, right?

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:06.900
Like, have the pause thing be, like,

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:09.540
enough to cover the field of action or something.

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:12.300
Like, I think it's just, it's incredibly tedious,

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:14.180
don't get me wrong. Like, it's definitely, like,

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.980
the game isn't encouraging it by any means.

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:18.600
But, you know, if anything, it draws out, like,

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.260
at least... I just can't believe people, like,

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:22.800
played like that enough to consider it, like,

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:24.040
a valid strategy.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:25.940
Yes, I know. Yeah.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:27.420
Well, if you think about it, though, right,

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:30.580
it's like, if you're trying to beat every NES game,

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:32.700
sooner or later, you're gonna come up with ways

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.180
to cheese any particular game

00:22:34.180 --> 00:22:35.440
that you're struggling with, right?

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.460
Like, we've figured out all sorts of things,

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:39.580
and I'm not encouraging this strategy by any means

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:43.360
because, like I said, it adds actually so much extra time to these fights

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:47.400
and it doesn't enhance the game at all

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:49.040
because, again, you're just saying now, like,

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:52.700
oh, cool, now I know I have to do, you know,

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:54.040
D-pad up and A.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:55.780
And that doesn't, like, to me,

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:56.680
that sounds like nonsense.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.000
So not something I would recommend,

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.060
but just a funny exploit

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:04.400
because it completely removes any actual challenge.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.520
It's almost like having an invincibility code

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:07.540
built into the game.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:10.440
Yeah, it's kind of like,

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:12.400
if you take away the live,

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.320
not live, but the action version,

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.240
the action element to this,

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:22.080
you're just, you're literally just putting in inputs

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:22.960
every couple seconds.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.800
You're like, all right, time to put in that next input,

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:27.620
unpause, put in the input, pause.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:29.080
All right, what's next?

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:30.140
Like, put in this input.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.200
You're not playing a game anymore.

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:34.980
It's not even, it's like worse than other types of cheating

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:37.780
because you're literally not even playing a game.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:40.260
You're just, you're just every couple seconds

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:42.900
putting in a new input that the game tells you to do.

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.900
You're playing keep talking and nobody explodes with the game.

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:47.980
Yeah, exactly.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:49.580
Except you can't really lose.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:53.440
Right, right. All right, I feel like that's enough

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:54.740
on the fighting game section

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:57.340
and we can move on to the side-scrolling levels.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:00.100
As I threw out a throwback episode

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:02.080
to Kid Nikki Radical Ninja,

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.340
do you guys even remember that game?

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:05.520
Nope. Yeah, that was the one you had,

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:08.020
you could go in different order of the levels, right?

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:10.360
Yes. Yeah, I remember that one.

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.160
Yeah, so don't remember that part,

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:14.700
but just the look and feel

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:16.520
of the side-scrolling action of it

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:18.180
is what I remember most from this.

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:22.880
But these side-scrolling levels are,

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:25.320
they're wraparounds. Yeah.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:26.940
You'll just constantly, you know,

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:28.400
it's like you're going on a treadmill

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:30.900
through the same set over and over and over again.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:32.420
And there are bosses.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:33.940
I don't know why they call them bosses.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:36.320
They might as well just be like enemies

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:37.820
with slightly more health

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:39.220
is what I would refer to them as.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.740
But the screen goes like dark for them,

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:43.680
which is kind of cool. It's like it makes it feel like

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:44.760
a little bit of a bigger fight.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:46.500
But these are the things that you experience

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:47.780
in these side-scrolling levels

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:49.520
is they're short, small stages

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.100
where you have to kind of figure out

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.920
where the bosses are,

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:55.240
defeat all of them,

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:57.040
where the hidden items are.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.440
And then lastly, once you've defeated all five bosses,

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:02.460
where's the key in the stage

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.000
so that I can unlock the door?

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:05.520
Well, all right.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.420
Let me give a little brief rundown of this.

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:11.760
Please. So there's no hidden anything.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:16.360
Like there's some hidden like health packs

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:19.420
and like many power-ups

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:21.280
in the statues that are littered

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:22.660
across the field.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:26.120
But the thing that triggers each

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:27.920
like actual power-up

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:29.800
and finding the key

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:32.380
to exit the side-scrolling level

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:34.200
is just kill five enemies in a row.

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:35.840
You kill five enemies in a row

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:37.220
and a boss comes out

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:38.980
and then you kill the boss

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:40.820
and you get the little piece of,

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.360
I don't know, kanji or whatever the Chinese equivalent is.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:47.420
And then you get your little power-up

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.280
or key and then you move on to the next one.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:50.780
You kill five more enemies in a row

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:51.920
and you fight the thing again.

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:52.940
You do it five times.

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:55.240
And that sounds like it takes a while,

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:57.440
but it doesn't because the game is incredibly simple

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:00.420
and easy in this mode.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:02.900
And then you just rinse and repeat

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:03.820
until you have everything

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:05.280
and then you just go to the door,

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:06.860
which is clearly marked as the door,

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:08.940
and you go to the next tournament.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.340
And my biggest question about this part

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:15.680
is why they even bother

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:17.400
contextualizing the things

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:19.080
that you're picking up as power-ups

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.520
because you have to get one,

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:23.500
you have to get them all in order

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.120
and then the key

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:27.460
in order to progress

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:28.880
and you don't get to keep anything.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:30.520
As soon as you get to the fight,

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:34.600
you are now in competition mode

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:36.620
and what you did in the side-scroller

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:37.460
doesn't really matter.

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.120
So it just felt tacked on.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:44.980
Just to do what you described earlier, Mike,

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.680
of just give you a break

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.820
from the more strenuous

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:51.800
one-on-one fighting,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.840
it just seems half-baked the whole time.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:56.680
Yeah, I didn't realize

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.860
that it was killing five enemies

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:01.260
that triggered the boss to come.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:02.920
But I did quickly realize

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:04.480
that I didn't have to actually move

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:05.540
anywhere in the level. I was like,

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:06.820
if I just stand here and fight for a minute,

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:08.700
I thought it was time-based or whatever,

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:10.100
but if I just stand here

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:11.500
and fight for a minute, the boss will show up.

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.220
So I guess I was killing five enemies.

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:14.720
But that also kind of made me think,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.020
what's the point of this whole level?

00:27:16.420 --> 00:27:17.560
I don't have to go anywhere.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:18.760
I can just stand right here

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:20.500
and everything I need will come to me.

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:21.760
I gotta find the door,

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:24.900
but that's fine. So it's not that big of a level.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.400
Usually the door is pretty early on.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:28.800
And then there's all this other stuff

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:29.720
that they put in these levels

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:31.380
that you don't need to go to.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:34.700
But Joe, just even now knowing

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.420
that I didn't have to explore the level,

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:38.680
I prefer the way you guys were playing it.

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:40.860
I think that it's such a chore

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:43.080
to play as this character

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:44.760
in these side-scrolling levels,

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:47.300
maybe specifically just because

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.760
you have to hit the up on the D-pad to jump,

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:51.680
and I just thought we were done with those days.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:53.400
But it's so bad here.

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:56.420
It's frustrating because the jump,

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:00.860
it's like you definitely can't do

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:02.140
multiple inputs at once

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:03.280
for whatever it's worth anyway.

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:06.340
But something about moving forward

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:08.560
and also having to jump,

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:11.720
because you have to, so you're holding right on the D-pad

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:13.380
and they can't register moving right

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:15.080
and jumping at the same time.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:16.700
But there's pitfalls

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.240
and there's platforms to get onto.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:21.080
That just made everything

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:22.760
infinitely worse.

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:23.600
And if I would have known

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:25.660
that I didn't have to move

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:27.400
from where my comfort zone was

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:28.380
and I could just stay there

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:29.900
and have everybody come to me,

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.140
I would have preferred that

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:32.640
because the worst part

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:35.960
about this part of the game,

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:38.520
the platforming, if you will,

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:41.720
just moving from the different layers

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:43.180
and things that you experience

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:44.140
throughout the level,

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:46.580
doing that over and over

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:47.440
and over again too,

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:49.740
because it's just a short loop

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:51.140
before you go back to the beginning,

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:52.660
doing that over and over again,

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:54.160
I wish I would have just not had

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:55.420
to have dealt with that

00:28:55.420 --> 00:29:00.000
or just put us in a tournament area,

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:01.720
but this time it's not

00:29:01.720 --> 00:29:03.820
with the martial arts rules.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.460
Now you're out there in the wild

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.340
so you can just attack

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:09.360
the way that you attack.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:12.100
It's like don't give me free range

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:13.040
to explore this thing

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:14.680
because I'm not interested in exploring it.

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:16.320
Yeah, I'm just going to clarify

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.660
that I think you only prefer it that way

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:19.560
because you didn't like

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:20.740
the experience at all.

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:24.140
I think if it was an engaging

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:25.160
part of the game

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:26.280
and you found out

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:27.800
there's a huge shortcut

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:29.580
that would actually bum you out.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:33.340
But yeah, just want to clarify that.

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:35.960
Yeah, but that's an interesting point too,

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:37.480
not even that I didn't enjoy it.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:39.220
It's an interesting point to mention

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:40.880
that the whole game

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:44.260
is just kind of rules-based on things

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:45.840
and we continue to find ways

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:48.520
to just exploit that very obviously.

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:51.720
Like they never actually designed anything

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:52.640
to your point, Sean,

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:54.700
of that it's just kill five enemies

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:55.700
and then the boss appears

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:58.400
and there's no like hidden area

00:29:58.400 --> 00:29:59.580
to find a boss

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:01.640
or hidden place to find a key

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:03.240
because it's just all like

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:07.140
you know, set up for you

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:09.700
in this structure over and over again.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:11.640
Whether it's the fighting levels

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:13.620
or the exploration levels,

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:16.520
you can just figure out

